{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1099500-qlippothic-spheres-for-everyone","id":1099500,"title":"Qlippothic Spheres for everyone!","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-06-02T07:05:03-07:00","id":1099500,"title":"Qlippothic Spheres for everyone!","content":"Through some unfortunate events, my current character gained an intuitive understanding of qlippothic spheres (without the whole Barabi Infernalist etc thing). In game terms, she automatically had half the dots (rounded up) in qlippothic she had in her regular spheres.<br /> <br /> Although dangerous, it had a neat flair to it. But as White Wolf never (and for good reasons) published a Nephandic source book, I was limited to the descriptions in the Book of Madness.<br /> <br /> In our campaign we took the 9 spheres (for several reasons like, it dividing reality into the smallest parts you can think of without having something as overpowered as space-time etc, paradox spirits, shard realms and so on) for cornerstones of the universe. This assumption makes it much easier to extrapolate to the qlippothic spheres.<br /> <br /> My ST and I came to the conclusion, that the qlippothic spheres theoretically do the same as the normal spheres, but (especially with the more squishy spheres like mind) certain things (destructive ones) are easier than others. <br /> <br /> Especially the first dot can be really interesting, since it gives the character knowledge on how things break apart, making it possible with, for example Qlippothic Matter knwoledge, to destroy something particulary sturdy or with Qlip. Mind, how to break someone most effectively.<br /> <br /> We defined normal spheres as Wyld magick, Qlippothic, as Wyrm magick and static magick as Weaver magick. (as the first being a purely creative power, the second leads things back to oblivion and the third being a result of reality and defined by stasis)<br /> <br /> As that, qlippothic magick, in metaphysical theory, makes things tear themselves apart (for example dissolving a fireball by directing the energies inside against another so everything dissolves into quintessence, flowing back into the universe. (You essentially act like the Wyrm itself, with the disadvantage of not being able to use this quintessence) <br /> <br /> This quintessence, tainted by the things that broke the former pattern apart, lingers as microquintessence in it's surroundings, explaining the strong resonances qlippothic magick leaves behind.<br /> <br /> An example in play: My character, an orphan mind mage, captured 4 Vampires and decided to indoctrinate them for her cause. She initially casted a Qlip Mind 4, Mind 4 Entropy 3 (you could as well not use Entropy 3 but it's her style) effect [With Qlip Mind 4: Dissolve the blood bond and existing loyalties inside their minds (Qlip being extra proficient in dissolving things) Mind 4 to let them think that her arguments are right and Entropy to just say the right things] as a ritual in which she talks to the chained vampire for a couple of weeks until they are broken, to rebuild them afterward)<br /> <br /> A thing you should note though, the powers of the Wyrm are not to be messeed easily. If she hadn't already been amoral, she wouldn't have been for long with using this powers. Falling is always a possibility and should tradition mages discover her dirty little secret, they probably would think she is fallen, or will soon.<br /> <br /> Also: it can give you an edge, but as alwys with mage, you have to use it creatively to get something out of it. And if you use it too much, the storyteller can always gift you with a quiet.<br /> <br /> What do you think of my idea? Do you like it, have something to add or think it is so EVIL you have to be at least a nephandus to use it? :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13530-queen-nihil","name":"Queen Nihil","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13530_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"21"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T07:35:24-07:00","id":1099512,"title":"","content":"Your system seems to work very well with the cosmology as laid out by your Storyteller. <br /> <br /> Infernalism: Path of Screams has a very quick section on Qlippothic Spheres which may be helpful, though it's less about mechanics than it is about the intent of the Mage.<br /> <br /> But I'm curious about what makes your character's sphere use &quot;Qlippothic&quot; as opposed to normal sphere use by way of a particularly entropic paradigm? (It reminds me a little of some Dreamspeaker paradigms that I've encountered, which view all of their sphere use through a spirit/umbral lens.) I'm interpreting your last comment to mean that you don't believe that a mage has to be completely evil or have evil intentions to use these spheres, so what is she doing when she accesses them? What does she think is happening when she calls on the Wyrm's power?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T07:50:19-07:00","id":1099517,"title":"","content":"The challenge with Qlippothic spheres is to make them something other than the normal spheres with Entropy added. Entropy 1 added to any of the spheres would do the same &quot;find the weak spot&quot; bit, no Qlippoth involved.<br /> <br /> My thought is that for each rank you have in the Qlippothic sphere, you can add a guaranteed success to the spell but the spell becomes automatically destructive and obvious - in essence, you're adding a wrecking ball.<ul><li>You are an Etherite with Qlippothic Forces 3. You are designing a Ignition Gun to set a target aflame, and you need it to be as good as possible. So instead of creating a field around the target that draws in ambient electromagnetics to create the flame, you set it to end the quantum spin of every atom in the vicinity to power it. Now the gun has free successes when you fire it, but it not only harms the person but also everything they're carrying as the quantum structure breaks down. And when your nemesis Dr. Electric reaches the place where you fired it, he'll easily see the remaining atoms with zero quantum spin, sticking out like a sore thumb.</li> <li>You are a Hermetic with Qlippothic Correspondence 3. You're being chased into your basement and need to teleport to the Chantry for safety. You really, really need this spell to work, and you left your sympathetic link to the Chantry in your bedroom upstairs. So instead of invoking Raphael the Archangel of guidance to carry you across the distance, you call upon Belial whose evil lives in all hearts and is thus in all places at once. You get free successes on the roll, but both the thugs running down your stairs and all your friends in the Chantry suddenly feel their soul shift slightly as you use it to <i>yank</i> yourself through.</li> </ul>Obviously this is overpowered as all hell. Mostly I think that's a feature rather than a bug; everyone in the setting is super-scared of Nephandi but it's never really clear why, and this makes it obvious. You could limit it by saying most <i>barrabi</i> only have the portion of their spheres they've relearned in Qlippothic fashion since visiting the Cauls, so while they can do a Forces 3 blast they can only &quot;befoul&quot; it by 1 because they only have Qlippothic Forces 1. Of course, that means <i>widderslainte</i> who've only ever learned Qlippothic spheres are especially scary...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T07:53:24-07:00","id":1099519,"title":"","content":"First of all, she is an Dominion, Yoga, Witchcraft Mind/Entropy Mage, although I began incorporating &quot;maleficish&quot; elements into it, especially when using qlip. magick. Her paradigm is everything is an Illusion with a pinch of a world of gods an monsters.<br /> <br /> She ist not evil, but essentialy being used as an experiment (essentially we wanted to play a bit with werewolf mechanics without not playing a mage. As it is essentially a one player campaign, (sadly, as there are just the two of us but mage works that way as well) my storyteller allowed her bastet forms equal to the Ceilican (as she is a artificial Shapechanger with a Housecat as feline form)) pre awakening, sawing the girl she had a crush on and two others of her &quot;family&quot; (her companions so to say) being killed by the technocracy kind off hardened her.<br /> <br /> She is not evil, but being literally &quot;essentially&quot; half cat diminished her human side a bit, alongside with many experiences. She views herself as a power of entropic/dynamic change, hating &quot;old weaver nets&quot; and as that, breaks down what she can't change.<br /> <br /> When she uses her, power, without wanting to serve anyone, she views herself as a force of the original wyrm (although her companions are worried what it could do to her). She gained her powers through contact with a lesser manifestation of the Eater of Souls. Understanding it's concept she thinks she just creates some free energy.<br /> <br /> When she casts, it's almost always coincidental, so if she uses her qlip. spheres, it's to break conceptions down through mind. An example of her power in play was, when she held a concert in which she used her music as an instrument to gently erode the banality of the local citizens, helping a friend getting elected as major and making life easier for changelings in her city.<br /> <br /> But use of qlip. magick is more exception than rule, reserved for destroying things.<br /> <br /> Edit: As her backstory could come across as being a true Shapechanger and it being about powergaming, she is limited to the forms, no gnosis, no rage [change with WP, comparatively easier, but somwhere there must be a system <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> (created with the bygone bestiary as a 10pt merit) and as I personally like not truly human characters, it seemed like a very interesting concept.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13530,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13530-queen-nihil","user":"Queen Nihil","date":". 06-02-2017, 11:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13530-queen-nihil","name":"Queen Nihil","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13530_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"21"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T08:01:28-07:00","id":1099524,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"7356\" data-vbnamecard=\"7356\">haldir2012</a> Quite the contrary. Nephandi try to be as coincidental as possible, casting most of their magick really low profile or not at all, just using emotional manipulation. The book of madness clearly states that qlippothic spheres &quot;tear reality appart&quot; and therefore attract much more paradox, should you be vulgar with it.<br /> Automatic successes make the system as you said too overpowered and misses the point of being the other side of the spheres.<br /> <br /> It would naturally be appropiate to for example need 1 success less to deflect your enemies fireball. Gain one automatic successs (like with forces for example) when using life to harm your enemy.<br /> My example. which you compared to entropy 1, was just for Qlip. 1 effects. If you go higher, the more is possible.<br /> <br /> Also, our concept wasn't to allow you things (or forbid you things) that aren't possible with sphere magick, but shift the focus. Of course you could just use Entropy 1 for that, which we both described, but you could argue that passive knowledge of Qlip 1 *could* have the same effekt.<br /> <br /> Also I could use regular mind to erode concepts in my enemies, but it's just easier with Qlip.<br /> <br /> Edit: Another thing to note, my intention was to make qlip. Spheres available to players to. Giving them this much power, (even though racking up excessive amounts of paradox) is not really what you want in your players hands. Automatic successes in mage are *really* powerfull.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13530,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13530-queen-nihil","user":"Queen Nihil","date":". 06-02-2017, 11:57 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13530-queen-nihil","name":"Queen Nihil","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13530_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"21"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T08:17:24-07:00","id":1099530,"title":"","content":"Sure - it just depends on what sort of Nephandi you want in your game. If you're running a Technocracy game and you want the Nephandi to be a creeping infestation of the Union, slipping under the radar and finding a home in the hardened hearts of your colleagues, then certainly my hack is way overpowered. If you want your PCs to encounter a single Nephandi deep down in the sewers and and you want them to quake in fear of this one guy, then my hack makes more sense.<br /> <br /> To reply to your edit - yes, this offers PCs a path to lots of mechanical power, but I don't think that disallows it as an option. Consider Vampire - any PC who can figure out a way to disable someone of higher generation can diablerize him and become more powerful, no XP required. In theory that PC could continue doing so until he has elder-level power and can kick everyone's ass in town. The limit on this isn't mechanical, it's social - somebody will notice the veins in your aura, somebody will notice all the missing ancillae, etc. Likewise here - if all of your effects break down everything nearby, people will start asking questions - and if you're a Tradition mage, you'll have both your colleagues as well as the local Technocratic squad on your ass.<br /> <br /> Finally - I like that it implies destruction is easier than the alternatives. To risk politics for a moment - it's pretty easy for a military superpower like the US to go into another country and blow stuff up. It's much, much harder to build a new country out of what remains, and it's also hard to modify the existing country to be what you want. I like the idea that most mages simply learn the &quot;harder&quot; version of spells - fireballs that don't drain all the energy in a 10-foot radius, or mental influence without leaving the target a drooling zombie - and part of becoming Fallen is figuring out that you can do a hell of a lot more if you don't care about the mess you leave behind.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7356,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","user":"haldir2012","date":". 06-02-2017, 12:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T09:21:27-07:00","id":1099545,"title":"","content":"I think maybe I asked the wrong question. I was more curious as to what would drive a simply amoral and cat-like person to use spheres that, through the very sparse information that we have in Book of Madness or Infernalism, encompass destructive principles considerably beyond the normal cycle.<br /> <br /> I mean, if you're just looking at the original balanced Wyrm ideology, that's probably represented reasonably well by the Euthanatos. Their entire philosophy revolves around using destruction and death to facilitate rebirth and creation anew. But they don't have to use Qlippothic spheres to do that.<br /> <br /> Infernalism seems to indicate that most Infernal mages at that time (it's a Sorcerer's Crusade supplement) don't really know that they're using Qlippothic spheres. That would imply that it doesn't necessarily require a conscious decision (as compared to say the Dark Side in Star Wars.) But they also basically state that mechanics-wise, Qlippothic sphere use should be pretty much identical to regular sphere use, just with &quot;malefic intent&quot;.<br /> <br /> It seems to work fairly well for the setting and cosmology that you and your storyteller have established, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-02T09:31:50-07:00","id":1099547,"title":"","content":"Ok I understand, or at least I think to. The easy answer would be opportunism and curiosity. It's a understanding she acquired and that also tainted her a bit. If you are an orphan, surrounded by enemies and (in her case after a long time of playing out different factions against each other, establishing herself as the leading witch with garou allies and Changeling vassals) having to protect a whole city, you try to gain every edge you can. <br /> <br /> Also, there always *is* some &quot;malefic intent&quot; in using these spheres. One time she simply utterly wished something to cease and it did (in a vulgar effett of course). When she is using these spheres, it comes out of hatred for the established, the things that don't change, that have to be broken apart so something new can be born.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13530-queen-nihil","name":"Queen Nihil","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13530_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"21"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-03T17:24:25-07:00","id":1099962,"title":"","content":"I'd scrap the Qlipphothic Spheres and play up the Jhor Resonance and the Morbidity form of Quiet. If I <i>had</i> to devise Qlipphothic Spheres, I'd go back to the first Book of Madness (?) And say that there's only Qlipphothic Entropy instead of a full suite of Qlipphothic Spheres.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T10:14:11-07:00","id":1100166,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1099500-qlippothic-spheres-for-everyone?p=1099962#post1099962\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd scrap the Qlipphothic Spheres and play up the Jhor Resonance and the Morbidity form of Quiet. If I <i>had</i> to devise Qlipphothic Spheres, I'd go back to the first Book of Madness (?) And say that there's only Qlipphothic Entropy instead of a full suite of Qlipphothic Spheres.</div> </div> </div> </div>Simplest is best. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-04T23:58:13-07:00","id":1100356,"title":"","content":"Everyone their sugaritos *g* WoD is anarchy, some like this nuance as it can benefit creative spellcasting, others view it as an clunky addition. <br /> <br /> My intent was to, similar to a Archmage thread that showed discontent by the official information, give players a somewhat homebrewish alternative.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13530-queen-nihil","name":"Queen Nihil","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13530_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"21"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-05T11:05:09-07:00","id":1100509,"title":"","content":"But, from your initial post, there is absolutely nothing to distinguish the mechanical uses of the &quot;Qllippothic&quot; Sphere from those that would make sense for someone with the exact same Spheres, similar Focus and the willingness to court certain types of Resonance (and the possible consequences thereof). Entropy 1 has ALWAYS been about breaking things down as a major component (same with Entropy 3, 4, 5, etc.) Her use as described broadly and from the example seems not so different. I guess it feels eeeeeeeevil/naughty/forbidden to the character and/or those with whom she hangs or something? <br /> <br /> The fact of the matter is that the Qlippothic Spheres have never really been anything special. They aren't even normal Spheres reskinned or set up with internal alternatives in a meaningful way, like Date or Primal Utility. Which is something of an outgrowth of the fact that Nephandi are really poorly defined as being something different from normal mages. By which I mean, Marauders ARE different. There are systems associated with that difference that actually matter to how the characters are perceived, how they function, etc. Decension hasn't been made meaningful in any similar way. It's just Ascesnion with chocolate sauce or nuts or something. Nor have Qlippothic Spheres. It's far to easy to overlap &quot;normal&quot; mages of various sorts whose Focus is nearly identical to those attributed to Nephandi, with the only difference being a subjective/game narrative of &quot;Oh, the Cauls! The Cauls!&quot; <br /> <br /> To me, this characters seems like she can be handled most simply by having her being drawn further and further towards certain types of Resonance and all that entails. It's not really anything other than the flavor of her magical practice that is changing. <br /> <br /> More complicated would be to have it be a shift in her Focus, but the rules on doing anything but abandoning Instruments (if you're the sort of mage that's into that) are vague to ethereal. It's more complicated because, if it's not just handwaved (which isn't really fair if any other character wants to do something similar), it's going to take some digging down and making up some new rules to graft on to Focus as written. (Though, to my mind, this would be &quot;not a bad thing&quot; and is probably what I would do in this circumstance...)<br /> <br /> Most complicated would be to figure out some mechanic like Marauder Quiet and make Nephandi qualitatively different from not-Nephandi/not-Marauder mages of other sorts. Good luck with that, but that does seem like the most promising area of all. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-05T22:41:15-07:00","id":1100717,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1099500-qlippothic-spheres-for-everyone?p=1100509#post1100509\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The fact of the matter is that the Qlippothic Spheres have never really been anything special.</div> </div> </div> </div>As Queen Nihil mentioned, according to The Book of Madness (Revised) they're described as permanently damaging reality in a way that Sphere magic typically does not. Like for Qlipspondence, the space in between the objects affected is partly erased, forever. Anyone subjected to a Qulipmind Effect will experience a permanent decrease in sanity--etc. That seems like a big difference to me. It seems to be kind of a fundamental feature actually, that goes beyond Resonance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","user":"Faradn","date":". 06-06-2017, 01:47 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-06T00:25:45-07:00","id":1100739,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1099500-qlippothic-spheres-for-everyone?p=1100717#post1100717\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> It seems to be kind of a fundamental feature actually, that goes beyond Resonance.</div> </div> </div> </div>On the other hand, the description of Qlippothic Forces seems to be lacking the &quot;wrongness&quot; and &quot;damaging reality&quot; parts - it is described as more of a different approach to Forces; one especially adapt at countering Forces effects of other Mages.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-06T00:41:45-07:00","id":1100744,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1099500-qlippothic-spheres-for-everyone?p=1100717#post1100717\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As Queen Nihil mentioned, according to The Book of Madness (Revised) they're described as permanently damaging reality in a way that Sphere magic typically does not. Like for Qlipspondence, the space in between the objects affected is partly erased, forever. Anyone subjected to a Qulipmind Effect will experience a permanent decrease in sanity--etc. That seems like a big difference to me. It seems to be kind of a fundamental feature actually, that goes beyond Resonance.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This ^^. It really depends on how you are viewing the information given on Nephandi and Qlippothic spheres. If you think it's normal magick with glitter, then my post obviously doesn't add anything interesting to your game. Sure, the *mechanics* are the same because... what *can* you really change without making them overpowered. The difference comes from consequence and roleplay.<br /> <br /> If you use Qlip. Magick you have to keep in mind (as it is described in Book of Madness Revised) that you are not changing reality, you obliterate tiny parts of it into oblivion. If you use qlip. Forces... well you have *destroyed* the pattern of energy (all without prime), leading the quintessence essentially to the lower umbra, where, depending on the cosmology you decide to use, it is forever gone, eaten by malfeans or whatever or just slowly pours back into the world.<br /> <br /> As that, there are some metaphysical appliances that are more intriguing to do with Qlip. magick then with normal sphere magick. Do you want to *erase* the importance of for example money, or a friend from someones head instead just &quot;overwriting&quot; it? The consequence could be that the afflicted feels that something is missing, but can't remember why.<br /> <br /> Edit:<br /> As the feeling &quot;it's not special enough&quot; seems to be the biggest reason for skepticism, qlip. Spheres are one of the things that are different by nuance (albeit a really important one). Really broadly by metaphysical origin, but not really in game terms, power level and so on. The Qlippoth, by definition it the hull of the sphere, it mirrors the opposite, just as zero mirrors infinity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13530,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13530-queen-nihil","user":"Queen Nihil","date":". 06-06-2017, 03:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13530-queen-nihil","name":"Queen Nihil","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13530_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"21"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-06T05:41:24-07:00","id":1100788,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1099500-qlippothic-spheres-for-everyone?p=1100509#post1100509\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which is something of an outgrowth of the fact that Nephandi are really poorly defined as being something different from normal mages.</div> </div> </div> </div>There are the Widderslaintes, whose Essences' descriptions really put across the idea that the Nephandi have different approaches to True Magick, than the Traditionalists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3254,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","user":"Muad'Dib","date":". 06-06-2017, 10:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-06T06:43:09-07:00","id":1100808,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Queen Nihil</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you use Qlip. Magick you have to keep in mind (as it is described in Book of Madness Revised) that you are not changing reality, you obliterate tiny parts of it into oblivion.<br /> ...<br /> Do you want to *erase* the importance of for example money, or a friend from someones head instead just &quot;overwriting&quot; it? The consequence could be that the afflicted feels that something is missing, but can't remember why.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have a problem with this, because it imples that Spheres cannot *erase* stuff. Destroying it's part of the methapysicall wheel that describes how Spheres work. Qilp could be geared towards greasing the way for that to happen, even to the point breaking the cycle and impeding the renewal of ambient quintessence, but it shouldn't be said that spheres just &quot;overwrite&quot; stuff, when they break them to the point of being nothing. If anything, you could say that the erasure of memory with Quilp has the side effect of reducing the capability of the person to remember stuff in the future, as the blank space in the memory can never be used again, unlike the more clean formatting of the normal Sphres.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 06-06-2017, 09:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-06T06:59:36-07:00","id":1100813,"title":"","content":"Ugh ^^&quot; close to giving up on that one but anyway... it's kinda hard to give an example for something that has to be &quot;felt&quot;. The point in qlippothic spheres is that zero is a mirror to infinity. You can do the same stuff, as I said. Spheres can also obliterate stuff into itzy bitzy parts. Qlippothic Spheres can also put things together.<br /> <br /> Is it easy to use the power linked to infinity to drive things towards zero? Without Prime it's not. Is it easy to use the power of zero to put something together? Most probably not.<br /> <br /> This is a thing of belief. If you think that the spheres are enough, yes. This thread is more than useless to you and you all have successfully wasted your time, congratulations! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> If you think that it adds nice *flair* and allows a character to understand the powers of oblivion *or* it helps you representing a nephandus magick, yay! I actually maybe benefited your campaign.<br /> <br /> If you are a pragmatist, you *of course* can say: &quot;Yeah, so mechanical wise it can pretty much do the same. Just use malefica and your evil intent is all there. Why not just use entropy, you are thinking to complicated&quot;<br /> <br /> But if you are convinced that they even *if they do (almost) the same stuff, it is fundamentally (different would be wrong) mirrored, here you are.<br /> <br /> If you still want to have this mirror and think my take on it is bullcrap, feel free to come up with something better, I would be thrilled.<br /> <br /> Thank you.<br /> (Even though I maybe phrased it all a little bit ruff, I don't want to offend anyone ^^&quot; just kinda frustrated with not being able to get my point across <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />)<br /> <br /> Also, I'm not nearly as knowledgable about the WoD as most of you guys and am terrible at getting informed about lore because it's not that interesting to me. I like dramatic stories, metaphysics and cool ideas. I read the book, guess I understood it and am ready to play xD","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13530,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13530-queen-nihil","user":"Queen Nihil","date":". 06-06-2017, 10:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13530-queen-nihil","name":"Queen Nihil","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13530_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"21"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-06T09:26:18-07:00","id":1100861,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1099500-qlippothic-spheres-for-everyone?p=1100717#post1100717\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As Queen Nihil mentioned, according to The Book of Madness (Revised) they're described as permanently damaging reality in a way that Sphere magic typically does not. Like for Qlipspondence, the space in between the objects affected is partly erased, forever. Anyone subjected to a Qulipmind Effect will experience a permanent decrease in sanity--etc. That seems like a big difference to me. It seems to be kind of a fundamental feature actually, that goes beyond Resonance.</div> </div> </div> </div>And that is something that is perfectly 100% totally and completely that any normal Sphere could do effectively. And if you REALLY want to hammer it home, there are Prime and Entropy effects that specifically deal with that. So, once again, there is nothing other than shear flavor text to make the distinction. Would you, as a ST, tell a player she or he could not eliminate the space or utterly wipe out the memory if their Focus and way of doing magic created a framework in which that makes total sense (of which I could reel off half a dozen with no problem)? Would you, as a player whose character was so configured feel that the ST was diminishing your character when you tried to do such a thing (which, once again, makes PERFECT SENSE according to how your character does magic) and gives you, as an excuse, that, no, you can't do that because that's something only Nephandi can do? When there is nothing out there but some stuff over here (a few statements on &quot;What Nephandi Magic Is&quot; which is directly in contradiction to all that huge pile of stuff over there (e.g. Mage magic and it's inherent flexibility as a whole)?<br /> <br /> Which, once again, is why the Marauders come across as genuinely different and the Nephandi are just &quot;bad mages&quot; that really aren't different from any other &quot;bad mages&quot;. There is a mechanical difference for the Marauders built entirely on one salient feature - the way Marauders handle Paradox, keying in on how they experience Quiet.<br /> <br /> Whereas, since Ascension per se is pretty flavorless (Another Arete = another die and that's pretty much it... compare to the way Foundations work in DA), so Decension is equally flavorless. And there is nothing beyond ST fiat or some really difficult overreaching/mental gymnastics on the fluff to make Qlippothic Spheres anything other that &quot;woo-woo bad&quot; Spheres that work just like their normal counterparts. Pretty much, the Nephandi are missing that key THING that makes them categorically different from the other mages. Which is 90%+ of the problem when any thread comes up discussing them. There is nothing that really keeps Gilles de Rais from being set up as a Hermetic or Chorister who is also just a very very very bad man who uses Maleficia as a Practice and all sorts of naughty things as Instruments as well as courting some absolutely awful Resonance willingly. There is nothing (besides the agreement of the players at the table) that keeps any PC from being just as bad as Gilles de Rais and still being a perfectly acceptable mage as far as being able to do magic, including &quot;magic of ultimate negation&quot;. Of course, there are usually some laws, ethics and social mores that are being shattered and there are social consequences, but there is nothing that is stopping them from doing that mechanically. As further proof of concept on that, just replace Gille de Rais with Voormas (or anyone from the Consanguinity of Eternal Joy) and the equivalencies are nakedly there. Which leads to all of these various sorts of discussions about how bad are the Nephandi, can one toy with being one and what does that mean, etc. And with the fact that those discussions inevitably fall apart in some fashion.<br /> <br /> So... as I said after Dataweaver's post... Simplest is best. The rules side can be kept VERY simple and the whole &quot;Dark Road She's Going Down&quot; is more of a story thing than it is a mechanical thing, because there just aren't any mechanics that essentially aren't just fluff behind a thin scrim of kinda-sorta-but not really rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1062,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","user":"Ajax","date":". 06-06-2017, 12:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-06T09:43:48-07:00","id":1100865,"title":"","content":"I think I have a different problem than <b>Ajax</b> above. I don't really see a problem with tweaking the mechanics for your Qlippothic spheres to emphasize the destructive elements. I think that fits the vague flavor text of the Book of Madness and Infernalism.<br /> <br /> My problem is that I think the concept is seriously diluted when you try to make it something for player characters. I think the flavor text makes it clear that Qlippothic spheres, like the Nephandi themselves, go beyond just standard &quot;I am evil&quot; bad guys. The concepts of the Nephandi, descension, and bringing the world back to Oblivion are very compelling when used well. But it's supposed to be about Oblivion. Scorched Earth or the Heat Death of the Universe. Actively pursuing the end of all things, and so on and so forth.<br /> <br /> If normal spheres are about manipulating the Tapestry, then Qlippothic spheres are about actively trying to unravel it. You've said that your character is amoral, not evil, that she believes in the cycle. Then there's no way that I could rationalize such a character actively using Qlippothic spheres. Because Qlippothic spheres are not intended to reflect natural empathy or facilitate the turning of the wheel. Qlippothic spheres are meant to kick the fulcrum out form under it. The cycle gets destroyed with everything else.<br /> <br /> That's why the books emphasize the idea of very little mechanical difference between them. Because it isn't about getting a slight advantage. It's because you're literally not supposed to be able to access these spheres unless you have the specific intention to not just destroy an object, but to unravel the fabric of reality.<br /> <br /> I'd recommend Infernalism, if you haven't read it, <b>Queen Nihil</b>, because the short section on Qlippothic spheres has a very quick look at each individual sphere and how the Qlippothic mage approaches them. And honestly, to me, there's no real way to rationalize using them unless the character is genuinely evil.<br /> <br /> But that said, your setting sounds like it has a number of unique elements, so if it works for you guys that's what matters. :-)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2430,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","user":"kalinara","date":". 06-06-2017, 02:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-06T09:46:53-07:00","id":1100867,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Queen Nihil</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1099500-qlippothic-spheres-for-everyone?p=1100744#post1100744\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If you use Qlip. Magick you have to keep in mind (as it is described in Book of Madness Revised) that you are not changing reality, you obliterate tiny parts of it into oblivion. If you use qlip. Forces... well you have *destroyed* the pattern of energy (all without prime), leading the quintessence essentially to the lower umbra, where, depending on the cosmology you decide to use, it is forever gone, eaten by malfeans or whatever or just slowly pours back into the world.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>There. That.<br /> <br /> Make that mean something other than just flavor text. <br /> <br /> And make it something meaningful that makes it &quot;worth it&quot; to go Nephandi. Make the Cauls actually do something other than just acting a &quot;reverse birth&quot; metaphor.<br /> <br /> Oh, and see if there might not be something that makes toying with the concepts of Nephandi-ness actually give you some reach into their idiosyncratic magical awfulness (like Infernal Investments only not because it's not little add-on superpowers but actually has something to with the mage and their magic). Perhaps with a nice little parallel system that would allow for mages that are Paradox-ridden, Quiet-prone, full of all sorts of chaotic (Wyld, if that's your thing) Resonance and/or insane can dip into Marauder-dom without taking that final step...<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-06T16:18:05-07:00","id":1101007,"title":"","content":"Anders Sandburg had <a href=\"http://evildrganymede.net/rpg/mage/m-fasoma.htm\" target=\"_blank\">some particularly evocative ideas about an interpretation of Qlippothic Correspondence</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-06T18:03:38-07:00","id":1101048,"title":"","content":"Sphere variants aren't just about adding or focusing on certain aspects of a Sphere (e.g., play up the destructive aspects). If that's all they did, they would all be overpowered. No, when you enhance certain aspects of a Sphere to better represent a given theme, you should also de-emphasize other aspects of that Sphere that run counter to that theme. If a Qlipphothic Sphere is inherently better at tearing things apart, it should also be worse at putting things together. If it's about 0 rather than \u221e, identify what sorts of Effects correlate to 0 and what sorts correlate to \u221e, strengthen the former, and weaken the latter. <br /> <br /> Look at Dimensional Science. It can do everything Spirit can do; but it's better when it comes to manipulating the Gauntlet and traveling to other Realms and it's not as good when it comes to manipulating EDEs. <br /> <br /> Sometimes, this shift of focus can be accomplished by trading levels: move shapeshifting effects down a dot in a Life variant while moving healing effects up a dot, and you have a variant that focuses more on change and less on stability. And so on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-06T18:29:44-07:00","id":1101060,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1099500-qlippothic-spheres-for-everyone?p=1100861#post1100861\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And that is something that is perfectly 100% totally and completely that any normal Sphere could do effectively. And if you REALLY want to hammer it home, there are Prime and Entropy effects that specifically deal with that. So, once again, there is nothing other than shear flavor text to make the distinction. Would you, as a ST, tell a player she or he could not eliminate the space or utterly wipe out the memory if their Focus and way of doing magic created a framework in which that makes total sense (of which I could reel off half a dozen with no problem)?</div> </div> </div> </div>The Spheres can do anything, but they don't do everything all the time. We're talking about a different version of Spheres that <i>always</i> destroy, and do so in concrete ways, which I already gave examples of. That is not &quot;flavor.&quot; The examples they have could stand to be expanded and made more rigorous, but the guidelines provide the ST an idea how a given instance of Qlippothic Spheres could damage reality.<br /> <br /> Edit:<br /> <br /> Maybe two different definitions of &quot;flavor&quot; are being used here. One of them is &quot;everything that is not strictly defined by a game system.&quot; That seems like a broad definition of flavor--but fair enough, the BoM doesn't give a detailed system for Qlipp Spheres.<br /> <br /> The other definition I'm seeing implied is &quot;mostly low-impact, like Resonance.&quot; I could not disagree more with that usage. The &quot;secondary&quot; impact described regarding Qlippothic Spheres is more significant than the spells themselves! Just teleporting something erodes the space between the origin and destination points, probably for good. That is clearly more significant than a teleport.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","user":"Faradn","date":". 06-07-2017, 02:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-07T09:55:27-07:00","id":1101274,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1099500-qlippothic-spheres-for-everyone?p=1101060#post1101060\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Spheres can do anything, but they don't do everything all the time. We're talking about a different version of Spheres that <i>always</i> destroy, and do so in concrete ways, which I already gave examples of. That is not &quot;flavor.&quot; The examples they have could stand to be expanded and made more rigorous, but the guidelines provide the ST an idea how a given instance of Qlippothic Spheres could damage reality.<br /> <br /> Edit:<br /> <br /> Maybe two different definitions of &quot;flavor&quot; are being used here. One of them is &quot;everything that is not strictly defined by a game system.&quot; That seems like a broad definition of flavor--but fair enough, the BoM doesn't give a detailed system for Qlipp Spheres.<br /> <br /> The other definition I'm seeing implied is &quot;mostly low-impact, like Resonance.&quot; I could not disagree more with that usage. The &quot;secondary&quot; impact described regarding Qlippothic Spheres is more significant than the spells themselves! Just teleporting something erodes the space between the origin and destination points, probably for good. That is clearly more significant than a teleport.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is EXACTLY flavor. It's limiting the expression of a Sphere in a way that is consonant with the magical practice of the user. It's no different AT ALL in any functional way from having the magic of most Hermetics &quot;having a Hermetic&quot; feel or that of Choristers having a &quot;Chorister-y way about it&quot;. If your Hermetic is doing his magic in exactly the same way most people would perceive as being associated with a Verbena with all the aspects of his Focus, then is he really a Hermetic other than socially? (By which he is going to be in Ex Misc, at best.... and is far to &quot;Diedne&quot; for most stogy Hermetic to be comfortable with even that).<br /> <br /> And the examples make no sense, really. So with Correspondence 2 I DESTROY (or erode or whatever) space? Fine. Nephandi win. I go to the North Pole and open a window the the South Pole and grab an Antarctic snow ball for shits and grins and destroy all the space between, as in, THE PLANET EARTH. (I could actually do it anywhere, but, hey, I'm a Nephandus,so I'll go with the epic trek to the pole to allow time for the &quot;heroes&quot; to interfere with my master plan.. BWAHAHAHAHA!) I guess, depending, I could just make the world into a double sided disk with the Equator for the circumference.... And maybe a hole at the center where the poles were.. So I made the Earth into a record.<br /> <br /> To make Qlippothic Spheres anything than utterly over the top (in both silliness or being too powerful or both) or something other than just a way to describe something ANY Sphere can do just exactly as well with slightly different fluff description you need mechanics to make that relevant.<br /> <br /> So, if you want your Correspondence 3 to &quot;erode space&quot; what, if not the silliness above, does that MEAN? Is it now shorter? Is it more perilous somehow? (E.g. There's a &quot;line of tripping&quot; that links the two spots? Cracks in the pavement? What?) And if it is meaningful how do you encompass that as being different than just skipping the space in any other way that someone? Can no one else teleport between those two locations now?<br /> <br /> As is, it's meaningless in any meaningful way and/or it's just as meaningful as any other flavor text for any alternative usage of Spheres.<br /> <br /> I guess you could make any use of a Qlippothic Sphere horrifically vulgar, like time travel vulgar, as utterly annihilating things is pretty hardcore..... (Though seriously, there are levels of Prime and Entropy that already do EXACTLY this... so if that's a free &quot;add on&quot; to any &quot;Qlippothic&quot; Sphere, I guess it's kinda like Do. Ridiculous amounts of extra stuff to allow for one specific type of mage for no reason and with no balancing effect when it could already be handled 100% by the core concepts of the Sphere system without any need for additional folderol... Yup, I'm doing this simple Matter 2 effect the NEPHANDUS way, so I get to add on some Prime 4 without having to have any Prime, be Arete 4, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1062,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","user":"Ajax","date":". 06-07-2017, 01:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-07T20:14:31-07:00","id":1101479,"title":"","content":"You're repeating yourself, contradicting yourself, and failing to meaningfully engage with my arguments. I'm out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}